Ontology & Knowledge Engineering Glossary (Plain Language)
Branch: A separate workspace in a GitHub repository where changes can be made without affecting the main version of a project.
Cellfie: A Protégé plugin that converts spreadsheets (Excel or CSV) into ontology content.
Commit: A saved change in Git/GitHub, like a snapshot showing what was added or updated.
Cross-Functional Collaboration: Working with people in different roles to solve shared problems.
Data Modeling: Designing how information is structured and connected.
Description Logic: The logical “grammar” behind many ontologies that ensures classes and relationships make sense.
Enterprise Ontology: Ontology used within a company to connect information across systems.
Formal Ontology: Ontology built using strict logic and rules for automated reasoning.
Git: A version control system that tracks changes and maintains history.
GitHub: A platform for storing projects, tracking changes, and collaborating using Git.
GitHub Actions: Automated workflows that run tasks such as validation checks.
GitHub Issues: A place to record tasks, bugs, and requests related to a project.
Interoperability: The ability of systems to share and understand each other’s data.
Knowledge Graph: A network of connected facts showing relationships.
Knowledge Representation (KR): A way of organizing knowledge so computers can interpret it.
MOBI: A digital book file format sometimes used for offline documentation.
Ontology: A structured map of concepts and relationships.
Ontology Governance: Processes for creating, approving, and updating ontologies.
Pipeline: An automated series of steps that process or transform data.
Protégé: A tool for building and visualizing ontologies.
Pull Request (PR): A request to merge changes into a main project branch.
Repository (Repo): A project folder stored in GitHub.
RDF: A standard way of representing information as linked statements.
ROBOT: A tool to automate ontology tasks such as validation and merging.
Scalability: A system’s ability to grow without breaking.
Semantic Consistency: Ensuring terms maintain the same meaning everywhere.
Semantic Data Architecture: Designing data systems so meaning is embedded from the start.
Semantic Interoperability: Different systems can share data and maintain the same meaning.
SHACL: Rules that check whether data follows the structure defined by an ontology.
SPARQL: A query language for asking questions within a knowledge graph.
Stakeholder Communication: Explaining concepts clearly to non-technical partners.
Subject Matter Expert (SME): A domain expert who helps ensure ontology accuracy.
Technical Documentation: Written explanations of how systems or ontologies work.
Triple Store / Graph Database: A database designed for storing relationships.
Use Case: An example showing how a system solves a real problem.
White Paper: A short report explaining a solution or design approach.
